## Lesson: no-git-stash-on-uncommitted-proof
Lesson ID: BL-20261003-no-git-stash-mid-round
Scope: Humanize RLCR worker loop / `solution.tex` editing workflow
Problem Description: While holding an entire round's uncommitted edits to
the single deliverable (`solution.tex`), a compile-vs-baseline comparison
was attempted via `git stash` + rebuild + `git stash pop`. The pop hit a
conflict against already-modified build artifacts, silently left the
working tree on the *baseline* `solution.tex`, and the only recovery point
(the stash) was then dropped — discarding the whole round's edits.
Root Cause: Using a stash/pop cycle to A/B a compile while the deliverable
has heavy uncommitted work; stash is not a safe checkpoint for this, and
the pop can silently revert content when artifacts are dirty.
Solution: Never stash mid-round. Before any compile-vs-baseline check,
commit the work to the branch (WIP commits are fine and amendable); commit
immediately after each batch of edits. Reversible-by-commit beats stash.
Constraints: Applies to single-file deliverables with a live RLCR loop; not
a general prohibition on git, only on stash/pop as a checkpoint mechanism.
Validation Evidence: Round-2 worker pass `/tmp/*.log` rebuilds; reflog and
`git fsck` showing the dropped dangling stash commit; full replay of the
patch sequence and the recovery commits `ccec7ee`, `d80b673`.
Source Rounds: appeared and solved in Round 2 (worker implementation pass).

